xhci: don't drop port events on a host with only a USB3 roothub

handle_port_status() ignores port events for the USB3 roothub when
shared_hcd is NULL, to cope with events arriving after the shared hcd
has been removed. But a host whose USB2 roothub has no ports runs with a
single roothub (xhci_has_one_roothub()): xhci-plat never creates a
shared_hcd, and the USB3 roothub is the primary hcd. On such a host
every port event is treated as stale and dropped, so the hub driver is
never told about a connection.

Broadcom Northstar (BCM4708/4709, e.g. Netgear R7000) is such a host:

  xhci-hcd 18023000.usb: USB2 root hub has no ports

A USB 3 disk on the front port trains to U0 at SuperSpeed (PORTSC
0x006a1203: Powered Connected Enabled Link:U0 PortSpeed:4, change bits
CSC WRC PRC PLC pending), the event ring holds its Port Status Change
Events, but the command ring stays empty and the device never appears.

Only skip the event when there really was a separate USB3 hcd to remove.
--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c
@@ -1985,8 +1985,13 @@
 	if (port->slot_id)
 		vdev = xhci->devs[port->slot_id];
 
-	/* We might get interrupts after shared_hcd is removed */
-	if (port->rhub == &xhci->usb3_rhub && xhci->shared_hcd == NULL) {
+	/*
+	 * We might get interrupts after shared_hcd is removed. A host with
+	 * only a USB3 roothub never has a shared_hcd: its USB3 roothub is
+	 * the primary hcd, so its events must not be dropped here.
+	 */
+	if (port->rhub == &xhci->usb3_rhub && xhci->shared_hcd == NULL &&
+	    !xhci_has_one_roothub(xhci)) {
 		xhci_dbg(xhci, "ignore port event for removed USB3 hcd\n");
 		bogus_port_status = true;
 		goto cleanup;
